Bounded segment plan, subject to planner schema ruling
Keep record canonical text, archive moves, identities, envelope bytes and Custody segment contract unchanged. Use separate per-position rows keyed [id,position] and tiny length metadata keyed id. An additive DB version2 can create segment-outcomes and segment-length while retaining the existing segments array store and both record stores untouched. Versioning fences old version1 writers; an old draft tab without versionchange handling may block upgrade, so fail visibly and require closing that tab. Never manufacture an empty segment from an old array or missing metadata. Detect old array keys with getKey/key cursor, without structured-cloning their full values. Planner must decide handling/recovery of any current-room draft arrays before this layout is implemented.

Validate safe integer positions/counts and bounds before opening a transaction. In one readwrite transaction, segment(id,at,list) reads tiny length, rejects gaps/at>length, deletes the [id,at..end] range natively, appends exactly the caller-owned outcomes at contiguous positions, and writes length=at+list.length. Transaction abort restores old tail and length. A repeated append at the same position cuts an interrupted tail as CustodyStore202-207 requires. Preserve the existing order where the segment may commit before the owning record; never invent record+segment atomicity. Repeated append repairs that interrupted tail.

segmented(id,from,limit) reads length and at most min(limit,length-from) position rows using a key range with count or a cursor stopped after the requested page. Verify contiguous keys/counts; a missing expected row, bad metadata, malformed outcome or legacy array is unreadable, not a shorter/empty history. No get(id) of a whole array, no getAll without a range/count, no reconstruct-all-then-slice. Current small-page reads and appends cost O(n) structured-clone/JS memory in history length n; new page read holds O(limit) returned outcomes, ordinary append O(k) JS memory for k input outcomes. Range truncation can still delete O(n-at) backing rows; do not claim constant total time for arbitrary truncation. The live-record listing remains an O(L) scan, L normally <=256 unresolved+64 terminal; archive retention remains unbounded by design. Do not add unrelated indices.

